  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Women Economic Empowerment in the Garment Industry in Pakistan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Deep-rooted gender norms and societal expectations in Pakistan limit women&apos;s participation in the workforce. Families and communities often discourage women from working, fearing reputational damage or workplace harassment. These cultural challenges are addressed during the project, and are actively mitigated by community engagement and sensitisation programmes to educate male family members and factory supervisors about the importance of women&#x2019;s economic empowerment.Ninety vulnerable women have been employed and provided with an income (minimum wage) in the three training centres in the supplier facilities. Until now, the technical curriculum has been able to train the batch of ninety women participants in cutting, sewing and quality control under the supervision of skilled female supervisors.</narrative>
  </description>
